# Pipeline-generated SQLite (regenerate with pipeline/phase3.mjs; the CSV
# sheets + *.json facts are the source of truth for historical stats)
data/*.db

# Phase 2's per-year scratch file. It writes one beside the merged facts on
# every run — the same rows the backbone and matchfacts files already hold, cut
# to one year. Nothing reads them; they are a byproduct of a rebuild, and ten
# of them appearing untracked after `npm run build:editions` is noise.
data/bourbon-cup-*-full.json

# Local Firebase project override (see .env.example). `*.local` above already
# covers .env.local; this catches a bare .env too.
.env

# Claude Code: settings.json and launch.json are shared so both developers'
# agents behave the same way. Personal permission grants stay out.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Firebase service-account keys. `scripts/import-history.mjs` needs one, and
# the console downloads it under this name — into whatever folder the browser
# saves to, which is sometimes this one. The key bypasses firestore.rules
# entirely, so a commit of it hands the whole database to anyone who reads the
# repo. Ignored by the pattern the console itself names the file with, in every
# directory, so it cannot be added by accident.
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
serviceAccount*.json

# ── The iOS build (see docs/app-store.md) ──
# `ios/` IS tracked, unlike the Android project, and the difference is that
# this one is edited by hand. Info.plist carries the usage strings and the
# export-compliance flag, App.entitlements carries push and Sign in with
# Apple, PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy is the privacy manifest, and the pbxproj holds
# iPhone-only and the entitlements path. Regenerating it would lose all of
# that; `ios/.gitignore` already covers the parts that ARE generated (the
# copied web build, the derived data, the config files Capacitor writes).
#
# GoogleService-Info.plist is not tracked. It is downloaded from the Firebase
# console and dragged into Xcode, which is also what creates its project
# reference — so committing the file without the reference would look present
# and do nothing. Without it the app builds and push silently never works.
ios/App/App/GoogleService-Info.plist

# ── The Android build (see docs/play-store.md) ──
# `android/` IS tracked, like `ios/` and like both of WBC's, because both are
# edited by hand — the signing block, the manifest, the launcher icons. What is
# NOT tracked is inside `android/.gitignore`: the copied web assets, the
# generated config, and the keystore.
#
# This used to be a bubblewrap TWA, whose generated Gradle project was a build
# artifact and correctly ignored. It is a Capacitor app now (see
# docs/play-store.md), so the project is source.
android.keystore
*.keystore
*.jks

# An APNs auth key. A .p8 is a real credential — it can send a push to every
# app in the team, since the one this project uses is team-scoped — and Apple
# hands it over EXACTLY ONCE, so a copy committed here is both a leak and the
# only copy somebody might then rely on. It belongs beside the keystore, not in
# a repo. Firebase → Cloud Messaging is where it goes.
*.p8

# The upload certificate exported from a keystore, for Play's upload key reset:
#   keytool -export -rfc -keystore <key> -alias <alias> -file upload_certificate.pem
# Not a secret — a certificate is the public half, and Play publishes it back —
# but it is a regenerable artifact of a key that is NOT in the repo, so a copy
# committed here would outlive the keystore it came from and read as current.
# Export it again when it is needed.
*.pem

# google-services.json is not tracked, for the same reason its iOS counterpart
# above is not: it comes out of the Firebase console per app, and without it
# native sign-in and push silently do nothing. See docs/play-store.md.
android/app/google-services.json

# ── Store screenshots ──
# `npm run shots:store -- --write` renders these from the LIVE tournament, so
# they are a photograph of a moment rather than a build artifact: a set taken
# today and a set taken next week differ by whatever the field did in between,
# and neither is more correct. Regenerate them when a listing needs them.
#
# Untracked-but-not-ignored was the worst of the three options — every
# `git status` on every machine showed `?? store/ios/`, and the first person to
# `git add -A` commits eight megabytes of PNG. It also hid the real hazard:
# both machines had a `store/ios` full of screenshots of a two-week-old UI,
# with nothing to say which was older.
#
# `store/play-feature-graphic.png` stays TRACKED, and the difference is that it
# is rendered from committed source — the mark and the two gradient stops in
# `public/BC ICON-01.svg` — and needs Montserrat installed on the machine to
# come out right. That one is reproducible; these are not.
store/ios/
store/play/
